) 10... O-O ) 7... Nf6 { Black develops the knight, responding to Qf3. } 8. Be2
{ White develops bishop. The instructor notes that if white plays Be2, black needs to know the trick: Ba3, and after bxa3, Ne5 with Qc3 and Qa1 ideas. }
( 8. O-O-O
{ White castles long, which is the main move. b5 fails here because of e5, and Nb5 is not possible because of Qa8. }
8... b5
{ Black plays b5. When f4/e5 comes, black should always be ready to play b4 - this is mentioned more than 10 times in the course. }
( 8... Ne5
{ Black retreats knight to e5 as recommended. After Qg3, b5 follows. } 9. Qg3
{ White plays Qg3 - the main point of Qf3 variation, trying to exchange queens for a slightly better position. }
9... b5
{ Black plays b5 after Qg3 as recommended. This is a key idea mentioned repeatedly in the course. }
10. Ndxb5
{ White captures with knight on b5. After axb5 Nxb5 Qb8, Bd4 d6 Bxe5 dxe5 Qc3 appears dangerous but black has Nxe4 - capturing material and giving the king a nice square on f6. }
( 10. Bxb5
{ Instead of Ndxb5, white can capture with Bxb5 which is not so dangerous but quite forcing. Since the knight is on c3, Rxa2 is not so easily available, but also Qc3 and Nc7 is not possible for white - pros and cons. }
10... axb5 11. Ndxb5 Qb8 12. Bf4
{ White plays Bf4. After Nh5, Ng3-Nb8 Knight h1 and Nc7 ideas. Bf4 d6 is the move recommended in the course. }
12... d6
{ Black plays d6 as recommended. The instructor says to find why Bxd6 is not good - not very difficult to find on your own. }
( 12... d6 { 431 } 13. Rxd6 { White captures on d6. } 13... Nh5 14. Qg5 Bxd6
15. Nxd6+ Qxd6 16. Bxe5 Ra5
{ Ra5 has a clear logic after Bxd6 - rook goes to g5. This is an important move. }
17. Nd5
{ Another 'funky' but nice move. f4 is also possible which was covered in the course. }
17... Qd7
{ Black plays Qd7. The position is quite messy with lots of tactics. If Qd8 instead, white can take on d8, Kd8 and Bc7 followed by Ba5 would not be pleasant. One must understand both sides' plans. }
( 17... Qd7 { 576 } ) ) ) ( 10. a3
{ On move 10, a3 is one of the main moves, stopping b4. The instructor mentions a painful defeat against Maxim Vachier-Lagrave in 2019 Bundesliga from this position. }
10... Bb7
{ Black develops normally with Bb7. Since the square b7 is occupied by the bishop, Bb6 is possible later - e.g., after axb5 Nxb5 Qb8 as black needs to support the knight, then Bb6 followed by Nc7 check. }
11. Bxb5
{ White plays Bxb5 - the point of the bishop move. After Bb6 followed by Nc7 check is the main idea. }
11... Rc8
{ Black plays the nice move Rc8. White needs to save the bishop on b5. } 12.
Ba4 { White saves the bishop. Be2 is also possible, covered in the course. }
12... Nxe4 13. Nxe4 Bxe4 14. Bf4 Qa5 15. Bxe5 Qxa4 16. Rd2
{ Even Rd2 is more or less forced here. The instructor believes black is better in this critical position. }
16... h5
{ The instructor's suggestion is to play h5. The main point is h4 h3, and Rg6 is another idea asking what white's next move is. This is the suggestion against this particular position with lots of nice ideas in the course. }
) ( 10. f4
{ 10th move f4 is by far the most played in this position. Apart from a3, this is another main line - the instructor considers a3 and f4 on move 10 to be the most challenging variations. }
10... Neg4
{ Black plays Ng4 immediately trying to exchange the bishop. Black's idea is to play b4, move the knight on c3, and Ne4 - a pretty standard plan. }
11. Bg1 { White retreats bishop to g1. Bd2 is another possible move here. } (
11. Bg1 { 909 } 11... h5 { 910 } 12. e5 { White pushes e5. } 12... b4
{ Black plays b4 - as mentioned many times, when e5 comes, b4 is the response. This takes the knight on c3. }
13. Nb1 { Knight retreats to b1. } 13... Ne4
{ Since the knight went to b1, black places his knight on e4. If the knight goes to a4, black can even go to d5. }
14. Qe1 Bb7
{ Black develops the bishop - called the 'bad bishop' in the course but actually a monster once it comes out. }
15. Nd2 Nxd2 16. Rxd2
{ These positions may be new in normal games but quite played in correspondence chess. Some ideas are quite interesting. }
16... Nh6
{ Black's idea with h5 and Ng4 leads to g6 Nh6 Nf5. Most of the time black aims for Nf5, gxf5 opening the g-file. The king is safer on e8 than anywhere else. }
17. Kb1 { White moves king to b1. } 17... g6
{ Black plays g6 as part of the plan to get Nf5. White has more space but the 'bad bishop' on b7 is actually a monster once it comes out. }
18. Bf2
{ White plays Bf2 - mainly aimed at Be7, wanting to play Bh4 to exchange the dark-squared bishop and claim better position due to weak squares. }
18... Bc5
{ Black does not play Be7 as Bh4 was white's idea. Therefore black goes Bc5, preventing white's plan. }
19. Bg1 Bf8 20. Bd3
{ Final move in Qf3 chapters: Bd3 instead of Bf2. Here Be7 is possible as g2 pawn is no longer protected by bishop on f1 since white played Bd3. }
20... Be7
{ Black plays Be7 - possible because the pawn on g2 is no longer protected by the bishop on f1 since white played Bd3. }
21. Bf2 Bxg2
{ After Bxg2, white doesn't have much compensation and black has a nice pawn structure - just a pawn up. }
) 11... h5
{ Black plays h5. The idea is to eventually get g6 Nh6 Nf5, and after Nf5 gxf5 opening the file for the rook. The king is safer on e8 than anywhere else. }
) 10... axb5 11. Nxb5 Qb8
{ Black plays Qb8 to protect the bishop on b7 and prevent the Nd6+ fork. } 12.
Bd4 d6 13. Bxe5 dxe5 14. Qc3 { White plays Qc3, which might appear dangerous. }
14... Nxe4
{ Black plays Ne4 - not only capturing material but also giving the king a nice square on f6 where it can hide. This is one of the key ideas black should know. }
15. Qc6+ Ke7 { King moves to e7, heading toward f6 for safety. } 16. Qxe4 Rxa2
) 9. e5
{ White pushes e5, attacking the knight on f6. b5 fails here as Ne5 is recommended after Qg3 and b5. }
) 8... Ba3
{ A trick black needs to know after Qf3 Nf6 Be2. After bxa3, Ne5 comes with ideas of Qc3 and Qa1. Not so difficult for white to see, but good to know. }
9. bxa3
{ White captures but this cooperative move leads to Be2 as the main continuation. }
9... Ne5
{ Black places knight on e5 after the a3 trick. The main move is Qg3 and now b5. }
